 "productionNotes": "No source consulted here publishes a worldwide production total for the third-generation Bluebird, and the figure most often repeated is a sales figure for one market rather than a build figure for the model. Nissan North America states that roughly 300,000 510s were sold in the United States between 1968 and 1973, and that in 1971 the 510 accounted for more than forty per cent of Nissan's American sales. That is a useful number and it is not a production total: Japanese, European, Australasian, African and South American output sits outside it entirely. Wikipedia, which aggregates widely, gives no total at all. productionTotal is left null rather than filled with an inference.\n\nThe shape of the run can be established where its size cannot. The range was launched in Japan in August 1967 as four-door saloon, two-door saloon and estate; the KP510 fastback coupe followed in November 1968 and was sold new only in Japan, knock-down kits to South Africa aside. North America took sedans and the estate but never the coupe. Assembly was not confined to Japan: Australian cars were either full imports or built up locally from Australian and Japanese parts, and New Zealand sedans were assembled under contract at Campbell Motor Industries in Thames at around forty per cent local content. British imports ran to roughly 4,000 cars from the October 1968 Motor Show, of which fewer than ten original UK-specification examples survive - a rate that describes the car's corrosion record more usefully than any production figure would. The 610 replaced it during 1972-73.",
